# TLS server, TLS shutdown after a complete header line
gnutls
#
# Companion to 2038, which stops the session while the first header line is still
# incomplete.  Here the header line is finished before the session goes, and that
# one byte of difference changes which part of the reading code sees the end of
# the session: a line still being assembled reports a connection lost while
# reading a header, whereas a line already complete reports one lost after a
# header.  The second is the case a reader can get wrong in the more damaging
# direction, because an end of file where the next header line could have begun -
# or could have been a folded continuation of the line just read - is what a
# message properly terminated by its last header also looks like.  Read as the
# latter, the message would be accepted, and acknowledged to the peer, with none
# of the body it declared: the peer is told the message arrived and so never
# sends it again.  Nothing may be accepted here.
#
# Both ways of sending a message reach it, so both are exercised.  Under BDAT the
# chunk is declared as 88 bytes and 22 are sent, so 66 are still outstanding when
# the session ends; under DATA the headers are simply never closed by a blank
# line and no terminating dot is ever sent.  Unlike 2038 the header line is sent
# without the ">>> " prefix, so the client appends the CRLF that completes it.
#
# The reply is matched in full rather than as just "421", for the reason given in
# 2038: the receive timeout answers with a 421 of its own, so a loose match would
# also be satisfied by a server that went on reading and then gave up waiting.
# The end of file after it is asserted for the same reason as there - a server
# that answered and then left the socket usable would pass on the reply alone.
#
# Run this by hand the same way as 2038, with the GnuTLS build handed to runtest
# and --tls left off:
#
#     cd test && ./runtest <gnutls-binary> --continue --test 2039
#
exim -DSERVER=server -DSRV=tls -bd -oX PORT_D
****
#
# Chunked body: the header line is complete, the chunk is not.
#
client-anytls 127.0.0.1 PORT_D
??? 220
EHLO rhu.barb
??? 250-
??? 250-SIZE
??? 250-8BITMIME
??? 250-PIPELINING
??? 250-CHUNKING
??? 250-STARTTLS
??? 250 HELP
STARTTLS
??? 220
EHLO rhu.barb
??? 250-
??? 250-SIZE
??? 250-8BITMIME
??? 250-PIPELINING
??? 250-CHUNKING
??? 250 HELP
MAIL FROM:<someone@some.domain>
??? 250
RCPT TO:<CALLER@test.ex>
??? 250
BDAT 88 LAST
To: Susan@random.com
stoptls
??? 421 Lost incoming connection
???*
****
millisleep 500
#
# Same again with DATA, which is the default way of sending a message and needs
# no CHUNKING at all.
#
client-anytls 127.0.0.1 PORT_D
??? 220
EHLO rhu.barb
??? 250-
??? 250-SIZE
??? 250-8BITMIME
??? 250-PIPELINING
??? 250-CHUNKING
??? 250-STARTTLS
??? 250 HELP
STARTTLS
??? 220
EHLO rhu.barb
??? 250-
??? 250-SIZE
??? 250-8BITMIME
??? 250-PIPELINING
??? 250-CHUNKING
??? 250 HELP
MAIL FROM:<someone@some.domain>
??? 250
RCPT TO:<CALLER@test.ex>
??? 250
DATA
??? 354
To: Susan@random.com
stoptls
??? 421 Lost incoming connection
???*
****
millisleep 500
killdaemon
#
no_msglog_check
